*bodies = (Body *)malloc(*nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  b = fread(bodies, *nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdin);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError reading input values\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n  return bodies;\n}\n\nvoid write_dataset(const int nbodies, Body *bodies) {\n  int b = fwrite(bodies, nbodies * sizeof(Body), 1, stdout);\n  if (b != 1) {\n    fprintf(stderr,\"\\nError writing to output\\n\");\n    exit(EXIT_FAILURE);\n  }\n}\n\nint main(int argc,char **argv) {\n  int nbodies;\n  Body *initial = read_dataset(&nbodies);\n\n  // Allocate two buffers for double buffering\n  Body *bodiesA = (Body *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  Body *bodiesB = (Body *)malloc(nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n  memcpy(bodiesA, initial, nbodies * sizeof(Body));\n\n  #pragma omp parallel\n  {\n    for (int iter = 0; iter < N_ITER; ++iter) {\n      // Determine source and destination buffers based on iteration parity\n      Body *src = (iter % 2 == 0) ? bodiesA : bodiesB;\n      Body *dst = (iter % 2 == 0) ? bodiesB : bodiesA;\n\n      #pragma omp for schedule(static)\n      for (int i = 0; i < nbodies; ++i) {\n        float fx = 0.0f, fy = 0.0f, fz = 0.0f;\n        // Use source buffer for all reads\n        for (int j = 0; j < nbodies; ++j) {\n          float dx = src[j].x - src[i].x;\n          float dy = src[j].y - src[i].y;\n          float dz = src[j].z - src[i].z;\n          float sqrd_dist = dx*dx + dy*dy + dz*dz + SOFTENING;\n          float inv_dist = 1.0f / sqrt(sqrd_dist);\n          float inv_dist3 = inv_dist * inv_dist * inv_dist;\n          fx += dx * inv_dist3;\n          fy += dy * inv_dist3;\n          fz += dz * inv_dist3;\n        }\n        dst[i].vx = src[i].vx + DT*fx;\n        dst[i].vy = src[i].vy + DT*fy;\n        dst[i].vz = src[i].vz + DT*fz;\n        dst[i].x = src[i].x + dst[i].vx * DT;\n        dst[i].y = src[i].y + dst[i].vy * DT;\n        dst[i].z = src[i].z + dst[i].vz * DT;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n\n  // Final state is in bodiesA if N_ITER even, else bodiesB\n  Body *final_bodies = (N_ITER % 2 == 0) ? bodiesA : bodiesB;\n  write_dataset(nbodies, final_bodies);\n\n  free(initial);\n  free(bodiesA);\n  free(bodiesB);\n  exit(EXIT_SUCCESS);\n}\n```\n\nThis is essentially double buffering with alternating arrays. It should be correct and likely similar to bda9a05217dd406c.\n\nTo differentiate and potentially improve performance, we could add `#pragma omp simd` on inner loop but I'll avoid due to correctness risk.\n\nMaybe we can use `schedule(static)` with a chunk size of 1? Let's try that: change to `schedule(static, 1)`. That would interleave i's across threads. Might improve cache behavior because each thread reads all bodies sequentially for its own i, but the src array is shared; interleaving might cause more cache line sharing? Not sure.\n\nGiven role C includes scheduling, I'll use `schedule(static)` default (which is contiguous) as it's likely best.\n\nAlternatively, we can use `#pragma omp parallel for schedule(guided)` which adapts chunk sizes. But static is usually better for uniform work.\n\nI'll keep static.\n\nNow, to make this a new experiment distinct from bda9a05217dd406c (which also used double buffering), maybe we add `restrict` qualifiers and use `#pragma omp simd` on inner loop but with reduction? Actually I can try adding `#pragma omp simd reduction(+:fx)` to the inner loop. This might vectorize and speed up, but could change order. However, many compilers implement SIMD reduction in a way that preserves order for small reductions? Not guaranteed.\n\nGiven the strict correctness, I'll avoid it.\n\nMaybe we can improve by using `#pragma omp parallel for schedule(static)` on the outer iteration loop with collapse? No.\n\nAnother idea: Use `#pragma omp parallel` and manually partition i's across threads without worksharing overhead. For example:\n\n#pragma omp parallel\n{\n  int tid = omp_get_thread_num();\n  int nthreads = omp_get_num_threads();\n  for (int iter=0; iter<N_ITER